\bottomrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize $^{a}$Representation-space only; not comparable to Cohen's $d$ (\S\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}\\
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize Empty ranks first in 6/8 suites total across both architectures.}\\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Representation-Space Sensitivity in NORA and Reference VLMs}
\label{sec:cf_repr}

NORA and reference VLMs lack action outputs; their sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale from Cohen's $d$).
NORA Goal produces saturated cosine distances ($1.0$); NORA Spatial peaks at $0.275$; reference VLMs show weak sensitivity ($\leq 0.19$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cf_details_appendix}).
The trained-reference contrast is consistent with action-space findings, though the different metric prevents direct magnitude comparison.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Inherited Routing: L1 Attention Profiling}
\label{sec:l1_priors}

L1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures reveal a pattern that contrasts with the training-associated effects documented in \S\ref{sec:r2_collapse} and \S\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}.
The TracVLA reference (Phi-3-Vision) allocates 32/32 heads to instruction-dominant attention across all four suites, saturating the capacity of its 32-head L1 before any VLA training.
Training slightly reduces this count to 27--30/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning does not increase L1 instruction-dominant attention in this architecture.
The NORA reference (Qwen2.5-VL) allocates 13--14 of its 16 L1 heads to instruction dominance (81--88\%), and training leaves this essentially unchanged at 12--13/16.
OpenVLA shows 24--26/32 instruction-dominant heads in the OXE-pretrained baseline; trained values are similar (14--24/32), with the largest change in Long-Horizon where training reduces the count from 25 to 14 heads (Figure~\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}).

If VLA training drove L1 instruction routing, reference VLMs should show low attention that increases after training, as observed in OpenVLA's random-weight condition (0/32; \S\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).
Instead, both reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate, and training leaves these counts unchanged or slightly reduced.
These results indicate that L1 instruction-dominant attention is largely inherited from pretrained backbones rather than created de novo by VLA fine-tuning.
\textbf{L1 instruction routing is an inherited backbone property, not a consequence of VLA training.}

\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\columnwidth}
\vspace{-8pt}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.44\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_attention.pdf}
\vspace{-4pt}
\caption{L1 instruction-dominant head counts (trained vs.\ reference). Medians across available LIBERO suites per architecture.}
\label{fig:cross_arch_l1}
\vspace{-8pt}
\end{wrapfigure}

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Synthesis: Training-Associated vs.\ Inherited Routing}
\label{sec:routing_synthesis}

The three analyses separate two empirical patterns: exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are training-associated (pronounced in VLM-to-VLA, small in VLA-to-VLA), while L1 instruction attention is inherited from reference backbones.
Despite qualitatively different reference profiles, trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peak decodability ($R^2 = 0.637$ vs.\ $0.634$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_arch_r2}), suggesting convergent representational reorganization.
These probes measure decodability, not causal reliance; with this caveat, the consistent three-way pattern provides a first cross-architecture decomposition of VLA instruction routing.

